on a crisp morning in june  snowflakes spread its wings and let the  strong wind of guadalupe island  help it take a first flight away from  its home  this wasn't the first time the young  black-footed albatross had soared above  the north pacific ocean  five months before as an egg scientists  flew snowflakes 6  000 kilometers from midway atoll in  hawaii  black-footed albatrosses can spend years  without touching land  but they return every year to mate and  nest in the islands where they were born  midway atoll makes up about one-third of  the global breeding population alone  there they nest on low-lying sandy  beaches  but this makes their eggs and chicks  very vulnerable to sea level rise  and flooding in 2011 a tsunami wiped out  about  30 000 of their nests on three atolls  the international union for conservation  of nature  lists the black-footed albatross as well  as their close cousin  the lacin albatross as near-threatened  since their breeding grounds are in  peril so are their populations  so scientists from mexico at grupo de  cologia  conservacion de islas and in the u.s  from pacific rim conservation had the  bold idea  to re-establish a population of  black-footed albatrosses  in a safer location scientists placed 21  eggs in incubators and flew them from  midway to guadalupe island  they traveled four legs on this journey  from private jets to domestic flights  on extra comfort rows the island is a  reserve  where black-footed albatross and other  seabirds once  nested feral cats introduced in the mid  20th century ended the colony but  scientists with hissy have been working  on the island for the past  20 years to eradicate invasive species  they've made progress but in the  meantime an excluder fence erected in  2014  keeps the southern part of the island  free of threats  after the long journey the midway eggs  met their foster parents  experienced lace and albatross pears  when  18 eggs hatched in february the new  parents fed  and cared for their adopted chicks  since scientists didn't know how well  lace and parents would raise the  imported eggs  they decided to also try the  translocation experiment with chicks  the team selected black-footed albatross  chicks and covered them at night  so they would not learn the hawaiian sky  and imprint there  the scientists repeated the journey  across the pacific in february  of 2021 after transporting the  one-month-old chicks to guadalupe island  jesse's team hand-reared them the chicks  were exposed to decoys and recorded  vocalizations of their species  they monitored the babies daily and fed  them until they were ready to fly away  all have done so translocations are not  first-line  interventions for saving species but  sometimes they're the only option  in the past 30 years a variety of  animals have been translocated  the conservation community hopes the  experiment with black-footed albatross  breaks ground for other similar projects  after the success of the lace and foster  parents the team is planning to bring at  least 80 more black-footed albatross  eggs to guadalupe island in the next few  years  but they won't know how well the project  works until snowflake  and the rest of the first batch return  in five  years to start looking for mates  when they do the scientists will be  waiting for them with open  arms  [Music]  you
 

